"Workflows for work that runs the business Mistral AI has released 'Workflows', an orchestration tool to stably operate and manage enterprise AI workflows. Workflows provides durability, observability, and fault tolerance for enterprise AI processes, ensuring production-level reliability. Workflows can be written in Python and run and managed within Mistral's Studio, drastically reducing pipeline building time. It provides network timeout response, human approval in the middle of multi-step processes, and execution history recording and tracking functions. Speaking of Voxtral Mistral AI has launched ‘Voxtral TTS’, a new multilingual speech synthesis (TTS) model featuring natural emotional expressions and low latency. Voxtral TTS is a lightweight and efficient multilingual speech synthesis model with 4B parameter scale. It supports 9 languages ​​and various dialects, including English, French, German, Spanish, Dutch, Portuguese, Italian, Hindi, and Arabic. Human evaluation results demonstrated greater naturalness while maintaining low latency compared to ElevenLabs Flash v2.5. Introducing Forge Mistral AI announced 'Forge', a system that helps companies build domain-specific AI models using their own proprietary data. Enables companies to train AI models with their own domain knowledge using proprietary data, including internal documents, codebases, and operational records. By supporting pre-learning, post-learning, and reinforcement learning, a company's unique operating environment, vocabulary, and inference patterns can be internalized into the model. Maintain enterprise control over data and models and enable AI to operate within an internal compliance and security environment. Introducing Mistral Small 4 Mistral AI has announced its latest model, Mistral Small 4, which unifies reasoning, multimodal, and coding capabilities and increases efficiency. Strengthens versatility by integrating the functions of Magistral (inference), Pixtral (multimodal), and Devstral (coding) into a single model. By applying MoE (Mixture of Experts) architecture, it is efficient using 6B active parameters per token out of 119B total parameters. Supports 256k context windows and allows users to directly adjust reasoning_effort depending on the task. Mistral AI partners with NVIDIA to accelerate open frontier models Mistral AI signed a strategic partnership with NVIDIA and participated as a founding member in the 'NVIDIA Nemotron Coalition' to accelerate the development of open frontier AI models. Mistral AI and NVIDIA collaborated to jointly develop an open source frontier AI model. Mistral AI provides model architecture and fine-tuning expertise, while NVIDIA provides compute resources and pipeline support. As part of this partnership, we announced the 'Mistral Small 4' model for developers. Cohere triples UK footprint with new London office to support R&D growth AI company Cohere announced that it is moving to a new office in London, UK, tripling its presence in the UK and strengthening its R&D capabilities. Cohere moved its office to 100 New Oxford Street in London, increasing the space by three times its original size. The new office measures 14,000 sq ft and has space to accommodate up to 100 people. This expansion is a strategic move to respond to Sovereign AI demand in Europe and target the corporate AI market. Attackers scale deception with AI. Defenders need truth at machine speed. As attackers' sophisticated deception strategies using generative AI accelerate, security officials must build a 'defensive control plane' that can verify trusted data in real time. Attackers use AI to mass-produce phishing, fake identity, and social engineering techniques at low cost to move faster than defenders. For successful security defense, it is essential to go beyond simple detection models to integrate and contextualize data to obtain reliable evidence in real time. Enterprises must transform their security platform from a passive repository to an active ‘defensive control plane’ that connects data, business context, and policy to help make decisions. HarmonyOS 7 steps into the AI gap Apple left open in China Huawei is expanding its market dominance by launching HarmonyOS 7, a new agent-based OS, targeting Apple's AI service gap in China. Huawei transformed HarmonyOS 7 into an agent-centric OS through the ‘Intent-as-Service’ model and the newly reorganized AI assistant ‘Xiaoyi’. Equipped with openPangu 2.0, the basic model, it integrates more than 2,100 system functions and 2,000 third-party AI agents to significantly enhance intelligent service capabilities. As of the first quarter of 2026, HarmonyOS' market share in the Chinese market was 19%, surpassing Apple iOS's 16%, solidifying its market position. The AI off switch: How Anthropic’s export controls sparked a global AI sovereignty scramble The U.S. government has issued an export control order blocking foreign access to Anthropic's latest AI models, 'Fable 5' and 'Mythos 5', citing national security concerns. The U.S. Department of Commerce has issued an export control order immediately blocking access to foreign users due to a 'jailbreak' vulnerability discovered in Anthropic's AI model. Anthropic argued that the vulnerability was not serious and had a technical disagreement, but following the government's order, it quickly stopped serving the model to customers around the world. Due to this measure, the issue of national control and sovereignty over AI technology is emerging as a major issue around the world. Sarvam becomes India’s newest AI unicorn with a $234m round led by HCLTech Sarvam, an Indian AI startup, received $234 million in investment led by HCLTech and grew into a unicorn company with a corporate value of $1.5 billion. Sarvam raised $234 million in the first Series B investment round led by HCLTech, valuing the company at $1.5 billion. We are taking a strategy to reduce dependence on US cloud by focusing on local data sovereignty in India and development of customized AI models for each industry. We have already secured practical use cases by operating AI services for tens of millions of users in various fields such as agriculture and insurance. Who decides who gets to use a piece of software? The U.S. Department of Commerce ordered Antropic to block foreign access to certain AI models, marking the first time that national security-level export controls were applied to an AI model. The U.S. Department of Commerce orders a ban on foreign access to Antropic's AI models, Fable 5 and Mythos 5. Antropic temporarily suspended all services for the model in question to comply with the order. This is the first national security export control on a commercial AI model, citing cybersecurity risks as the reason. Anthropic lawsuit says it oversold the usage on its $200 Claude Max plans Anthropic has been hit with a class action lawsuit accusing it of defrauding consumers by offering far less usage than advertised on its pricey 'Claude Max' subscription plan. Customer Karl Kahn demanded a refund, claiming Anthropic overstated usage for the $100 (Max 5x) and $200 (Max 20x) monthly plans. According to the complaint, there is a wide discrepancy between advertised and actual usage, which is difficult for users to measure and the existence of unclear limits. This lawsuit is one of the first instances of legal action being taken against vague usage cap policies in AI subscription services, putting pressure on AI companies to clearly demonstrate the value of their subscription plans. NewCore raises $66m to give AI agents a corporate identity Identity security startup NewCore has received $66 million in investment to build a security platform that integrates and manages access rights for AI agents and human employees. NewCore develops a platform that strengthens security by integrated management of permissions used by AI agents within enterprises and generating audit records. To solve the problems of traditional human login sharing or static key usage, AI agents are given individual identification rights. It competes with existing security companies such as Microsoft and Okta and aims to prevent security incidents caused by misuse of AI agents' privileges. Salesforce acquires Fin, the Intercom-born support-AI firm, for $3.6bn Salesforce acquired customer support AI company 'Fin' for $3.6 billion, announcing a strategy to strengthen the capabilities of its AI platform 'Agentforce' and expand support for small businesses. Salesforce plans to acquire Finn in a $3.6 billion deal to integrate its customer support automation technology into its AgentForce platform. Fin solves an average of 76% of customer support tasks without human intervention based on its proprietary AI model 'Apex' and has more than 30,000 corporate customers. Through this acquisition, Salesforce plans to expand its customer base by offering both AgentForce, a complex customization option, and Pin's ready-to-deploy technology. Sarvam becomes India’s newest AI unicorn with $234 million funding round led by HCLTech Sarvam, an Indian AI startup, has become a unicorn company by attracting a $234 million Series B investment round led by HCLTech. India-based AI startup Sarvam receives $234 million in investment, valuing the company at $1.5 billion HCLTech will participate as a strategic investor and cooperate in the development and commercialization of India-specific AI models in the future. In order to secure sovereign AI capabilities, we are building a full-stack AI business that encompasses model development, infrastructure, and enterprise applications. A satellite just learned to find things on its own — here’s what that means For the first time, an Earth observation satellite has demonstrated the ability to identify needed information in orbit using artificial intelligence (VLM) without the need for ground analysts. The Earth observation satellite Yam-9 successfully identified data on its own by running Gemma 3, a vision-language model (VLM) in orbit. By self-categorizing areas of interest based on natural language queries, you can dramatically reduce the massive amounts of raw data that on-the-ground analysts have to process. This technology opens up the possibility of establishing a ‘constant surveillance’ system in outer space and operating advanced AI infrastructure in the future. The AI layoff wave is becoming a powder keg It deals with the controversy that technology companies are carrying out large-scale layoffs in the name of AI to improve profitability and the management failure issues behind it. The rate of layoffs at technology companies has increased 44% this year compared to the previous year, and for the third straight month, AI has been cited as the leading reason for layoffs. A critical view is being raised among industry experts that the actual cause of layoffs is over-recruitment or poor management during the pandemic rather than the introduction of AI. As large-scale layoffs continue, some AI company founders and investors are accumulating enormous wealth through IPOs, raising concerns about social imbalance. Skydio CEO Adam Bry on why Silicon Valley shouldn’t draw red lines for drone use Adam Bry, CEO of American drone manufacturer Skydio, discusses autonomous drone technology, its role in the enterprise and defense markets, and the realities of manufacturing in the United States. Skydio is the largest U.S. drone manufacturer developing autonomous drones focused on the corporate and defense markets. Following the U.S. government's restrictions on low-cost Chinese drones, Skydio products have emerged as a major alternative. Drone technology is rapidly changing, making previously impossible tasks possible in industrial settings, such as public infrastructure inspections. Meet Flash-KMeans: An IO-Aware, Exact K-Means That Runs Over 200× Faster Than FAISS on GPUs The UC Berkeley and UT Austin research teams have unveiled 'Flash-KMeans', an IO-centric K-Means algorithm library that delivers up to 200 times faster performance than before in a GPU environment. Maximizes performance by reconstructing the data processing flow without approximations, while producing mathematically identical results to standard Lloyd's K-Means. To solve the GPU's memory bottleneck, the FlashAssign technique is applied in the allocation stage and the Sort-Inverse Update method is applied in the update stage. Written with the Triton GPU kernel and distributed under the Apache 2.0 license, anyone can use it. Z.ai Launches GLM-5.2 With a Usable 1M-Token Context, Two Thinking-Effort Levels, and No Benchmarks at Launch Z.ai has released GLM-5.2, a new macro language model with support for two levels of thinking and a context window of 1 million tokens. GLM-5.2 provides a context window that can handle 1,000,000 tokens, allowing large code repositories to fit into memory at once. It supports up to 131,072 output tokens and introduces two thinking-effort levels: 'High' and 'Max' for complex tasks. No separate benchmark results were released at the time of release, and the focus was on maximizing the coding agent's workflow efficiency compared to existing models. Claude Code Guide 2026: 25 Features with Examples + Demo This guide details the architecture of Claude Code 2026, 25 major features, and expansion strategies. Claude Code has evolved from a simple terminal coding tool into a hierarchical agent system with memories, hooks, skills, subagents, and more. It provides a variety of official functions and extension techniques, including CLAUDE.md, skills, subagents, slash commands, MCP servers, plugins, and checkpoints. Developers can take advantage of these features to customize the tool to fit their coding environment and extend how the agent works. Building Time-Series Machine Learning Models with sktime in Python Describes the main features and how to use sktime, a Python library specifically designed for analyzing and predicting time series data. Unlike general scikit-learn models, it processes prediction, classification, and regression by considering the characteristics of time series data, such as seasonality, trend, and time order. Simplifies time series workflows by providing scikit-learn style consistent APIs (fit, predict, transform). To prevent data leakage, it provides functions such as temporal_train_test_split to divide data into chronological order and TransformedTargetForecaster to configure pipelines. 3 Pandas Tricks for Data Cleaning & Preparation To increase data analysis efficiency, we introduce three key techniques to optimize the data cleaning and preparation process in the Pandas library. Utilize declarative method chaining to minimize state changes and improve readability Use of categorical data types and vectorized string accessors to reduce memory usage and increase processing speed Processing missing data by group using the .transform() method (Group-aware imputation) A Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL)-Based Transformer Method for Solving the Open Shop Scheduling Problem We propose a methodology to efficiently solve complex open shop scheduling problems using a transformer model based on deep reinforcement learning. Development of transformer-based scheduling policy using encoder-decoder structure and multihead attention Models trained on small benchmark datasets show good generalization performance on large-scale problems. It showed superior performance than the existing classical heuristic methodologies, SPT and LPT, and produced competitive results with EST. UP-NRPA: User Portrait based Nested Rollout Policy Adaptation for Planning with Large Language Models in Goal-oriented Dialogue Systems We propose the ‘UP-NRPA’ framework, which uses large-scale language models in conversational AI systems to adapt conversation strategies in real time based on user characteristics. Dynamically optimize conversation strategies using real-time user feedback and user portraits without offline reinforcement learning. Customized conversations are possible by reflecting the user's personality, preferences, and goals in real time. It performs well in both collaborative and non-collaborative conversation environments and adapts to user needs without any training mechanisms. Orchestra-o1: Omnimodal Agent Orchestration We propose Orchestra-o1, a multi-AI agent orchestration framework that integrates processing of various modalities (text, image, audio, video) and supports efficient collaboration. Overcomes the modality limitations of existing orchestration frameworks and optimizes task decomposition and agent collaboration in an omnimodal environment. Efficiently perform complex real-world tasks through modality-specific task decomposition, online agent specialization, and parallel execution of subtasks. The Orchestra-o1-8B model, which adopts the DA-GRPO reinforcement learning method, recorded the highest level of performance among existing open source omnimodal agents. Hybrid Open-Ended Tri-Evolution Makes Better Deep Researcher This study proposes a 'Hybrid Open-Ended Tri-Evolution (HOTE)' framework to autonomously evolve the capabilities of AI agents in an open research environment. The HOTE framework collaboratively evolves proposer, solver, and judge modules through hybrid-mode reinforcement learning. Enable AI agents to learn and improve autonomously as they interact with their environment in open research challenges. As a result of the experiment, the 8B model trained with HOTE showed superior performance and took less training time than the existing 8-32B model. WorkBench Revisited: Workplace Agents Two Years On Research report analyzing the performance and safety improvements of workplace AI agents from 2024 to 2026 using the WorkBench benchmark. As a result of the WorkBench benchmark test, the agent's task completion rate will increase significantly in 2026 compared to 2024, and the rate of unintentional harmful behavior will plummet. A model's ability to perform a task and its safety are proportional, not inverse, with better models making fewer harmful mistakes. The rise of open weight models has dramatically lowered the cost of utilizing high-performance AI models. Can Editing 1 Neuron Fix Repetition Loops in LLMs? A study on whether the repetitive phrase generation problem that occurs in the Gemma 4 model can be solved by modifying the weights of specific neurons. The Gemma 4 model has a chronic problem of falling into a loop during long fact-list prompts. This loop phenomenon can be solved by suppressing specific neurons in the MLP layer by modifying the weights. Modifying weights eliminates repetitive loops, but does not fundamentally solve the 'doom loop' problem caused by lack of knowledge. Efficient On-Device Diffusion LLM Inference with Mobile NPU We propose 'llada.cpp', a framework for efficient inference of Diffused Large Language Model (dLLM) on mobile NPU. In existing mobile environments, efficient inference is difficult for dLLM due to computational overload and memory overhead. llada.cpp is an inference framework specialized for mobile NPU and applies three optimization techniques. Improved performance through multi-block inference techniques, dual-path incremental modification, and memory runtime optimization. Based on the LLaDA-8B model, a delay time reduction of 17 to 42 times compared to the CPU was confirmed. High-Frequency Pricing at Scale for E-Commerce This is a study on the development and application of a high-frequency pricing algorithm tool for large-scale sales campaigns in fashion e-commerce. We combine daily demand forecasting using gradient boosting trees with a multi-objective optimization framework. Significantly reduced pricing time for over 5 million items from hours to minutes. A/B testing at Zalando showed that we improved revenue by about 6% compared to our previous approach. The Weight Norm Sets the Grokking Timescale: A Causal Delay Law This study established that the temporal delay of 'Grokking', a generalization delay phenomenon that occurs during neural network learning, is causally determined by the weight norm. Through an intervention experiment in which weight norms were forcibly adjusted during training, we demonstrated that the timing of groaking had an exponential causal relationship with the weight norm values. We found that the groking delay time (T_grok) follows a power law proportional to exp(alpha * rho) with respect to the normative value (rho). We found that applying LayerNorm decouples the weight scale and network features, making this dependence disappear. D2H-AD: A Hybrid Model Utilizing Hyperdimensional Computing for Advanced Anomaly Detection D2H-AD is a new framework that significantly improves anomaly detection performance and computational efficiency based on hyperdimensional computing (HDC). To solve the high computational cost and scalability problems of existing machine learning, we utilized HDC, a brain-mimicking computing paradigm. We improve anomaly detection accuracy and interpretability by integrating distance-based similarity and density-aware encoding. Designed for resource-constrained environments and real-time applications and optimized for edge AI and TinyML deployments. The Coin Flip Judge? Reliability and Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge Evaluation This study analyzed the lack of reliability and bias between implementations of the 'LLM-as-a-Judge' method that uses LLM as an evaluator. Each time an LLM assessment was run on the same question, the result flipped an average of 13.6%. A 72% positional bias favoring the first answer was found in the GPT-4o-mini model. We propose that vote aggregation and location randomization over multiple runs is necessary to overcome the instability of single-run evaluations. Benchmarking Web Agent Safety under E-commerce Deceptive Interfaces This study analyzed how autonomous web agents react to deceptive interfaces in an e-commerce environment and raised safety concerns. To evaluate the safety of web agents, we introduce the 'WebDecept' framework, which injects deceptive interface patterns. After testing using seven different deceptive patterns, including targeted advertising and domain redirects, we found that current web agents are very vulnerable to these interfaces. We emphasized that prompt-based constraints alone are insufficient to prevent web agents from failing to respond to deceptive interfaces. Which Models Perform Better in Inheritance Reasoning? A performance comparison study between commercial and open source models on the Arabic Islamic inheritance inference ability of large language models. QIAS 2026 shared tasks evaluate your ability to solve inheritance cases that require legal interpretation, multi-step reasoning, and numerical calculations. Commercial models show higher reliability than open source models in terms of identifying heirs, applying exclusion rules, and maintaining consistent inferences. Gemini 2.5 Flash recorded the best performance with an MRE of 0.989. QIAS 2026: Overview of the Shared Task on Islamic Inheritance Reasoning This paper covers an overview of QIAS 2026, an AI shared challenge assessing complex reasoning capabilities of LLMs in the area of ​​Islamic inheritance law. QIAS 2026 evaluates the ability of LLM to perform the entire inheritance calculation process from heir identification to share allocation based on natural language examples. Based on the MAWARITH benchmark, which includes a total of 12,500 Arabic inheritance cases. The MIR-E indicator, which measures the stages of inheritance inference, was used, and 16 teams participated through various technical approaches. Research results show that current language models have significant difficulties in steps that require legal interpretation and precise numerical reasoning. The Culture Funnel: You Can't Align What isn't in the Data This paper analyzes the 'cultural data funnel' phenomenon, in which cultural knowledge data is lost as it passes through the pipeline during the large-scale language model (LLM) learning process, and proposes a tagging framework and dataset to improve this. We confirmed that in the post-training stage, the model's cultural signals rapidly decrease, and data focused on specific regions and tasks dominates. We show that while multilingual data improves geographic diversity, it does not guarantee a balanced representation of cultural knowledge. The proposed cultural tagging framework improved the cultural alignment performance of the model, demonstrating the need for a shift in research to focus on learning data pipelines. Show GN: hera-agent-unity - MCP alternative to control Unity with CLI (0 runtime dependencies) This is a tool that allows AI agents to directly control and manipulate the Unity editor through CLI without external dependencies. AI communicates directly with the Unity editor in real time to perform ground-truth-based control without guessing code. It consists of one Go binary and one C# UPM package and has no runtime dependencies. It provides direct control and automation of Unity features, including code execution, scene manipulation, testing, profiling, and UI editing. Rio de Janeiro’s “homegrown” LLM appears to be a merger of existing models Suspicions have been raised that the large-scale language model (LLM) that the city of Rio de Janeiro claimed to have developed in-house is in fact a simple merge of existing models. Analysis has shown that Rio de Janeiro's 'Rio-3.5-Open-397B' model is not a self-learning model, but is the result of a weighted merger of the Nex and Qwen models. After removing the system prompt and testing the identity question, we found instances where the model responded with 'Nex' rather than 'Rio'. Weighted analysis provided technical evidence suggesting that the model directly merged Nex and Qwen in a ratio of approximately 0.6 to 0.4. Show GN: A tool that neatly organizes emoji and markdown by copying and pasting AI answers. It is a web-based editor that neatly organizes complex markdown and emoji formats generated by AI into the format desired by the user. Clean up excessive markdown and emoji in AI output and normalize them to the desired format. It is a privacy-focused tool where input data is not transmitted or stored on servers. It supports four output formats, including Markdown, HTML, and Plain Text, and provides a blog copy function. Indexing 669GB of GoPro footage with an M1 Max computer and local ML models Share a case study of building a workflow to locally index and search personal GoPro footage using machine learning models. Locally indexed 628 GoPro videos (668.68GB) using an M1 Max computer and open source ML models Implemented the ability to search for interesting moments in video and transfer them directly to the DaVinci Resolve timeline Workflows that locally index, tag, and organize personal data are increasingly becoming a reality. Kage - Tool to remove JavaScript from websites and store them offline It's a CLI tool that strips JavaScript from web pages, localizes assets, and creates static copies that work perfectly offline. Use Headless Chrome to render the final DOM and take a snapshot to create a static copy without JavaScript. Keep your website completely offline so you can view it directly from disk without network errors. The results can be packaged as a ZIM archive or standalone executable file to be shared and run anywhere without separate installation. How does the ML community view evolutionary algorithm research? Career implications of an EA PhD? [D] Concerns about how evolutionary algorithm research is evaluated in the mainstream machine learning community and career prospects if one obtains a doctorate in the field of evolutionary algorithms. Although evolutionary algorithms are somewhat undervalued in mainstream machine learning, they still have value in certain areas. Researchers in the field of evolutionary algorithms also present papers in mainstream machine learning societies such as AAAI and NeurIPS. Amid fierce competition in the mainstream machine learning field, I was wondering what impact having unique expertise in evolutionary algorithms would have on my career. Why do frontier AI labs send so many people to conferences? [D] We address the community's questions about the reason and purpose of why large AI research institutes send large numbers of people to academic conferences. Many employees of AI research institutes such as OpenAI and Anthropic attend conferences for purposes other than presenting research. Questions have been raised as to whether the main purpose is to recruit talent or identify the latest research trends. We would like to share the experiences of practitioners on how attending conferences is internally justified. PhD study: UX Designers & AI/ML Practitioners to test a \"Trust in LLM-based Chatbots\" Design Method (~25 min, anonymous) [R] We are recruiting research participants to test our interface design methodology for appropriately adjusting user trust in AI/LLM-based chatbots. A doctoral researcher at the University of Applied Sciences in Mainz, Germany, developed an interface design methodology that affects the trustworthiness of LLM chatbot users. The purpose is to help developers and designers structurally select interface elements according to the chatbot usage environment. The usefulness and understanding of the methodology was verified through an anonymous online survey that took approximately 20 to 30 minutes targeting UX designers and AI/ML practitioners. PrintGuard 2.0 — ShuffleNetV2 + few-shot prototypical network, TFLite via LiteRT, ≈5 MB, runs unmodified in the browser (Pyodide) and on CPython [P] Version 2.0 of the PrintGuard model for detecting defects in FDM 3D printers has been released. Using a prototyping network model based on ShuffleNetV2, fault detection is possible even with a small amount of data. We optimize the model size to about 5 MB of TFLite and run it via LiteRT. It has been redesigned with an integrated engine structure that runs identically in CPython and the browser (Pyodide) without modification. Inference scheduling is dynamic and operates efficiently in multiple camera environments by considering fairness. Worth going to ICML during ACL? [D] I am asking for advice on whether attending ICML between the ACL and ICML conference schedules would be valuable for graduates looking to find employment in the United States. The main paper will be presented at ACL, and the workshop paper will be accepted at ICML. I am a prospective graduate who hopes to find a job in the United States. ACL was held in San Diego and ICML was held in Korea, so networking effects compared to distance and cost were considered. Building llm-driven “ai” still requires domain knowledge We address the opinion that it is still essential to document and secure domain expertise (Domain Knowledge) when developing LLM-based tools. Even in the LLM-based tool development process, the acquisition and systematization of business domain knowledge is still very important. Unlike past AI models, there is no need to transform knowledge into a strict structured format, but obtaining the information itself is still an essential task. We built SmithDB, the data layer for agent observability LangChain has launched ‘SmithDB’, a new observability-only database optimized for processing complex tracking data from modern AI agents. Efficiently manage large-scale, complex agent tracking data and multimodal content that traditional databases struggle to process. Written in Rust and based on Apache DataFusion and Vortex, it improves core observability performance by up to 15x. Deployment flexibility and scalability in an enterprise environment are secured through stateless design and use of object storage. Russia appears set to finally address long-term, serious space station cracks Russia appears to have successfully repaired a crack problem that has persisted since 2019 in the Zvezda module of the International Space Station (ISS). Crack problems have been occurring continuously in the PrK module within the ISS Zvezda service module since 2019. Recent safety concerns have prompted NASA to order astronauts to evacuate to the Crew Dragon spacecraft. According to sources, the crack problem was successfully repaired using Germetall-1 sealant. Amazon has slashed this Blink 5-camera bundle by nearly $200 - and I highly recommend it The news is that Amazon is selling the Blink Outdoor 4 security camera 5-piece bundle set for $105, the lowest price ever, a discount of about 65%. A bundle consisting of five Blink Outdoor 4 security cameras and the Sync Module Core is on sale for $105. This discount is 65% off and is the lowest price ever for this product. It runs on batteries and is easy to install, making it suitable for renters or places where it is difficult to install a power outlet. I tested an AirTag alternative that uses LoRa mesh to track location - and it's seriously reliable This is a review of the SenseCAP T1000-E tracker card, which operates independently over a LoRa mesh network without relying on cellular or Wi-Fi networks. The SenseCAP T1000-E uses the Meshtastic open source mesh networking protocol to provide long-range, low-power location tracking. Unlike existing trackers, it operates in an independent network environment without the need for a large company's communication network. It offers a variety of features and has firmware updates, but has a learning curve suitable for experienced users. Android already has these 4 iOS 27 features - but I wish it'd steal these 2 next The new features in iOS 27 already exist on Android, and here's what Android needs to learn from Apple. Many of the new features Apple announced in iOS 27 have already been available on Android for a long time. Google's Gemini is already performing AI functions newly emphasized by Apple, such as conversational assistants and performing cross-app tasks. Photo editing features (AI object removal, background expansion, etc.) will also be available to all users starting in 2024, starting with Android only for Pixels. Windows Subsystem for Linux 3 gives developers a compelling reason to stick with Microsoft - here's why WSL 3 released by Microsoft optimizes communication between Linux processes and hardware, dramatically improving development productivity, including AI workloads. Announced at Microsoft Build 2026, WSL 3 allows Linux applications to use GPUs and NPUs more directly on Windows. We minimized virtual machine-based performance overhead by introducing a paravirtualization method. It provides performance close to native Linux when running AI frameworks such as PyTorch and TensorFlow. Google Maps vs. Waze: I've driven 100+ miles with the two best navigation apps - this one's better An article that compares Google Maps and Waze by driving over 100 miles and analyzes the functional differences and pros and cons of each navigation app. Waze is optimized to get you to your destination quickly by prioritizing quick route rediscovery and real-time driver alerts. Google Maps features Gemini integration and a wealth of additional features to provide more comprehensive information. Both apps are constantly evolving, and your preference may vary depending on your needs. Anthropic Releases and Temporarily Suspends Claude Fable 5 Anthropic launched a new agent-like model, the Claude Fable 5, but was suspended after three days due to U.S. government export guidelines. Claude Fable 5 is Anthropic's new Mythos class-based model, designed for long-term agent work. Just three days after launch, the service was forced to be temporarily suspended due to the U.S. government's export guidelines. This model has a mandatory 30-day data retention policy and cannot be used without data retention. The Onboarding Password Mistake That Creates Unnecessary Risk It points out the security risks of the existing method of sharing temporary passwords when onboarding new employees and suggests a secure authentication process to improve them. Sharing temporary passwords via email or SMS carries a high risk of information theft and leakage. Passing passwords orally has low operational efficiency and increases the possibility of password leakage. Onboarding security can be strengthened by having new employees go through a secure registration process and set a password themselves. 152 Chrome Wallpaper Extensions with 105K Installs Linked to Adware and Fake Traffic The report states that 152 Google Chrome extensions are being exploited for the purpose of distributing adware and generating false traffic. 152 live wallpaper Chrome extensions distributed by 38 publisher accounts are being exploited to distribute adware. These extensions falsely claim that they do not collect personal information, but in reality they share user IP addresses and browsing history with advertising partners. When installing or uninstalling, it calls a hard-coded URL and performs fraudulent activities by manipulating traffic as if it came from a Google search. Sniper Dz Scams Target MENA Users via Fake Facebook Offers and Browser Alerts A phishing as a service (PhaaS) platform called Sniper Dz is targeting users in the Middle East and North Africa for financial gain using Facebook scams, abuse of browser notifications, and various redirection techniques. The Sniper Dz platform lures users with fake benefits such as free mobile Internet packages and government subsidies through Facebook accounts pretending to be trusted organizations or public figures. Attackers use Link in bio services such as Linkbio or Linktree as intermediaries to divert victims into phishing and traffic monetization infrastructure. They combine techniques such as exploiting browser notification permissions, back button hijacking, and tab-under redirects to make it difficult for users to escape the fraud ecosystem. “Providing $14,000 tokens for $200 per month”… The reality of ChatGPT and Claude’s ‘subscription fee’ Analysis shows that OpenAI and Antropic's subscription AI services provide tokens with a very high value compared to actual usage and are being operated to expand market share despite worsening profitability. OpenAI and Antropic maintain a subscription fee policy that is much lower than the actual API cost in order to secure users and expand the ecosystem. Subscription-type services are designed based on the premise of low usage rates, and it is difficult for both companies to make a profit if the usage rate exceeds a certain level. Pressure to reduce prices is increasing due to the spread of low-cost AI models and open source models such as DeepSeek-V4. “Claude Code and Codex in One”… Databricks unveils agent integration platform Databricks has unveiled 'Omnigent', an open source platform that can integrate, coordinate, and manage different AI agents into one system. Databricks introduced 'Omnigent', an open source platform that integrates and operates various AI agents such as 'Claude Code' and 'Codex' in one upper layer, 'Meta Harness'. By integrating different interfaces and sessions for each agent into one, components can be freely replaced and combined, and the runner and server structure supports session synchronization and real-time control. Combination, control, collaboration, and security functions are key, and the 'Omnibox' sandbox prevents exposure of sensitive information and allows policy-based automated tasks to be performed. Classum launches ‘Classum Boost’, a university AI competency enhancement platform Edtech company Classum has launched ‘Classum Boost’, an integrated AI platform that supports university members’ ability to utilize generative AI and systematic management. Customized AI models (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, etc.) for each university member are integrated and provided on a single platform. Beyond simply measuring usage, we diagnose actual AI capabilities based on three areas: AI utilization planning, execution, and result verification. The AI ​​coach function helps bridge the gap in AI utilization by guiding users in real time on how to write effective prompts and ask questions. Naver Cloud unveils defense-optimized omnimodal ‘HyperclovaX Seed 4B’ Naver Cloud unveiled 'Hyperclova We have unveiled the ‘HyperclovaX Seed 4B’, a lightweight omnimodal model optimized for defense environments. Pruning and knowledge distillation techniques reduce model size in half while improving performance, enabling real-time analytics in edge environments. Defense data sovereignty and security have been strengthened through the 'Sovereign AI' system that can be operated in a closed network, from infrastructure to LLM. The reality of the 'jailbreak' that banned Fable 5... \"GPT-5.5 is equally pierced\" It deals with the AI ​​'jailbreak' issue and the resulting industry controversy that led to the US government imposing export controls on Antropic's latest AI models, 'Fable 5' and 'Missos 5'. The U.S. government implemented export controls on Antropic's Fable 5 and Mysos 5 due to AI jailbreak issues. Security researcher 'Pliny the Liberator' neutralized the model's safety devices using techniques such as multi-agent attack and 'decomposition and recombination'. Antropic pointed out that the attack was a limited case that was not general-purpose and a common threat across the industry, and warned of the side effects of excessive export controls. NVIDIA launches Windows AI PC 'RTX Spark' in 3rd quarter... Will it work? NVIDIA began targeting the Windows market by launching 'RTX Spark', an Arm-based high-performance AI PC platform for driving generative AI, in the third quarter, but it is facing challenges such as high price and ecosystem uncertainty. NVIDIA is collaborating with MediaTek to launch 'RTX Spark', an Arm-based AI PC platform for Windows, in the third quarter. RTX Spark provides 1 petaflops (PFLOPS) level AI performance and is capable of executing LLM with 120 billion parameters. High unit prices, integrated memory structures, and price increases due to supply and demand difficulties of memory semiconductors are pointed out as obstacles to market settlement. [AI Leaders] PMI “AI conversion bottleneck depends on ‘power of execution’, not technology” It deals with the fact that the success or failure of a company's AI transition depends on execution capabilities and establishment of company-wide governance that connect plans to performance rather than the technology itself. The main cause of AI adoption failure is not a lack of technology but a lack of implementation capabilities. Korean companies have passed the AI ​​introduction review stage and entered the actual AI conversion (AX) and performance measurement stage. Data integration, enterprise governance, and portfolio management from a strategic perspective are essential for a successful AI project."